  "text": "The megalithic civilization, also called the megalithic culture, was a pre-Celtic culture. Across all the Celtic lands stand structures built from rock. A pre-Celtic people built them, a people whose name and culture are now unknown. The lack of written records obscures them. The name comes from the megalith, a term for a large stone. This civilization flourished around 5000 B.C.E. Megaliths range from a single upright standing stone, the menhir, to groups that form a passage grave, a dolmen, or a cromlech. Stone circles may surround or include dolmens and menhirs. The structures range from small to huge. Stonehenge in Britain and Brú na Bóinne in Ireland stand out among them. The stone circle at Lough Gur is the largest. Many monuments carry symbols read as sunbursts, stars, and water meanders. Some archaeologists see a consistent language in them and read the sites as goddess temples. That theory is controversial. Two facts about the builders are beyond dispute. They were skilled builders, and they raised the oldest remaining human structures from rock. They also had deep knowledge of the stars. Many monuments point precisely to a constellation, a moon phase, or a sun position. The burial mound at Newgrange admits the winter solstice dawn through a stone transom. The line of stones at Carnac points to the sunrise on the same day. The open circle at Stonehenge faces the summer solstice dawn. The cairn at Loughcrew opens to both equinoxes. The megalith builders were not Celts, and the Druids did not design their monuments. The Celts used the great stones for their own ceremonies, and the sites passed into folklore as fairy-haunted places. Christian councils from the fifth century onward denounced worship of the stones, yet many monuments still stand in the Celtic lands today.",
  "objective": "Document the megalithic civilization, the pre-Celtic builders of great stone monuments.",
